  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "Some people have two hands, they can press two keys at the same time.\n\nPress of a new key shouldn't cancel out the one pressed before it.\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Press and *hold* the \"h\" key, and *prepare* to press the \"i\" key with your opposite hand.\n\n2. Press and *hold* the \"i\" key with your opposite hand, while the \"h\" key is still being held.\n\n3. Release the \"h\" key you *pressed earlier*, while the \"i\" key is still being held.\n\n4. Once \"h\" is released - you will notice the letter \"h\" did not appear in the input field. (because it got interrupted by the press of the \"i\" key.)\n\n5. Release the \"i\" key.\n\n6. Once \"i\" is released - you will notice that \"i\" made it into the input field because it's the last \"non-interrupted\" key. \n\nVideo: https://youtu.be/ZdTTZlGxqRw\n\nVideo explanation: I attempt to press a second letter while the first one isn't released. Pressing a second letter cancels out the press of the first letter. Before I pull away the controller from a letter I make sure to release a trigger (which normally would cause the letter to show up)\n\nEdit: Replaced video link because it went unavailable. Added \"Steps\" and \"Video explanation\".",
